          "text": "{\"regions\":{\"r2\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"latter viz. 12\u00bd years is passed in childhood and old age\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"each. ( In computing this the night-time is not taken into\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"consideration, and the day is supposed to be of 24 hours as\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"usual; this makes the 12\u00bd years equivalent to 25 years for\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"boyhood and for old age. ) Mr. Telang suggests that \u0924\u0938\u094d\u092f\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"\u091a\u093e\u0938\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0927\u0938\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0927\u092e\u094d should be taken to mean \u0930\u093e\u0924\u094d\u0930\u093f\u0917\u0924\u0947\u0924\u0930\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0927 (i. e. \u0926\u093f\u0928\u0917\u093e\u0924\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0927)\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"\u0938\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0927\u092e\u094d i. e. half of the 50 years passed by day time. This gives\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":9,\"text\":\"25 years for youth and old age together. \u0905\u092a\u0930\u0902\u2014may be taken\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":10,\"text\":\"as in the com. or as an adv.; besides, another thing is that &c.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit compounds in lines 6 and 8 are partly obscured.\"},\"r3\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 51. The comparison of man with an actor pervades the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"whole verse. \u0915\u093e\u092e\u0930\u0938\u093f\u0915\u0903\u2014delighting in enjoyments. \u0938\u0902\u0938\u093e\u0930\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0947\u2014\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"at the close of his worldly existence. \u092f\u092e\u0927\u093e\u0928\u0940\u0966\u2014\u092f\u092e\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0927\u093e\u0928\u0940,\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"\u092f\u0948\u0935 \u091c\u0935\u0928\u093f\u0915\u093e a curtain; as an actor having acted his part in\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"several ways, retires behind the curtain on the stage, so man\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"retires to the seat of death from the platform of the world.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit at the start of line 4 is obscured.\"},\"r4\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 52. \u092a\u094d\u0930\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e refined or acute intellect. \u0905\u092d\u093f\u092e\u093e\u0928\u2014just pride.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"\u092e\u093e\u0928\u0926\u2014may mean (1) \u092e\u093e\u0928\u0902 \u0926\u0926\u093e\u0924\u093f ( \u0938\u094d\u0935\u0947\u092d\u094d\u092f\u0903 ) \u0907\u0924\u093f; one who confers\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"honour ( on his dependants ); and (2) \u092e\u093e\u0928\u0902 \u0926\u094d\u092f\u0924\u093f \u0907\u0924\u093f from\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"\u092e\u093e\u0928 + \u0926\u094b ( \u0905\u0935\u0916\u0923\u094d\u0921\u0928\u0947 ) one who subdues the pride ( of the enemy\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"or insults others ); the second sense, however, seems to be\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"intended here. \u090f\u0915\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0924\u0903\u2014totally, utterly.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null},\"r5\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 53. The same idea is rendered clearer in this. \u0935\u092f\u0902\u2014\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"The use of the pl. shows that the speaker wishes to assert his\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"own greatness. \u092f\u093e\u0935\u0926\u0930\u094d\u0925\u092e\u094d\u2014\u092f\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0928\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0903 \u0924\u093e\u0935\u0924\u094d \u0964 we are the sole masters\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"of the wealth of words i. e we rule supreme over the domain of\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"letters. \u0917\u093f\u0930\u093e\u0902\u2014The gen. by the rule \u0905\u0927\u0940\u0917\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0926\u092f\u0947\u0936\u093e\u0902 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0923\u093f ( \u0937\u0937\u094d\u0920\u0940 ) \u0964\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"P\u00e2n. II. 3. 52. Words meaning to remember. or remembrance\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"and the roots \u0926\u092f\u094d and \u0908\u0936\u094d govern the gen. of their object.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Some Sanskrit forms and diacritics are unclear.\"},\"r6\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 54. \u0926\u0941\u0915\u0942\u0932\u0948\u0903\u2014is a better reading than \u091a \u0932\u0915\u094d\u0937\u094d\u092e\u094d\u092f\u093e as it gives a\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"good contrast with \u0935\u0932\u094d\u0915\u0932\u0948\u0903. \u0928\u093f\u0930\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u094b \u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0903\u2014Ramarshi explains\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"as follows:\u2014\u0905\u0924\u094d\u0930 \u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0903 \u0905\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0902 \u092d\u0947\u0926 \u0907\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0903 \u0964 \u0928\u093f\u0930\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0903 \u0905\u0928\u093e\u0927\u093f\u0915\u094d\u092f\u0903 \u0964 \u0905\u0924\u094d\u0930\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0947 \u0905\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0902 \u0928\u093e\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0940\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0903 \u0964 The philosopher says to the king\u2014what\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"you regard as greater satisfaction is not so in my estimation.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"Mr. Telang does not think R\u00e2marshi\u2019s explanation to be satis-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit reading at the start of line 6 is partly obscured.\"},\"r1\":{\"type\":\"page_number\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"( 66 )\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null}},\"units\":[{\"id\":\"u1\",\"type\":\"notes\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"u2\",\"u3\",\"u4\",\"u5\",\"u6\"]},{\"id\":\"u2\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"50\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r2\"]},{\"id\":\"u3\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"51\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r3\"]},{\"id\":\"u4\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"52\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r4\"]},{\"id\":\"u5\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"53\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r5\"]},{\"id\":\"u6\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"54\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"r6\"]}],\"flows\":[{\"flow\":\"main\",\"units\":[\"u1\"]},{\"flow\":\"notes\",\"units\":[]}],\"relations\":[]}"
